{"step_index":250,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-24T07:27:18Z","model":"gemini-3.8-flash-high","input_tokens":2267,"cache_read_tokens":121230,"output_tokens":2949,"thinking":"The undo feature requires handling two distinct scenarios. One involves undoing a player's move after the AI's response, necessitating a rollback of two plies. The other involves interrupting the AI's calculation, requiring an abort of the AI process and a rollback of one ply.\n\n\nUndoing a move involves resetting the game state. This includes handling game-over conditions by reopening the game, restoring the clock, and removing the game-over status before reverting moves. After reverting, the Stockfish engine's internal state must be synchronized to match the board position using the `position fen` and `isready` commands.\n\nThe hint feature requests the best move from Stockfish using the current board position's FEN, without modifying the game's history. The response will include the move's `from` and `to` squares, SAN notation, and a principal variation for display.\n\nMove history will use standard algebraic notation displayed in a two-column format. Clicking moves will allow viewing the board at that point. Additionally, a PGN export function with copy and download options will be available.\n\n\nPlayer color selection introduces the need to immediately trigger an AI move if the player selects Black, inverting the standard game flow. The board view must also automatically adjust to reflect the selected player's color, or allow user control over flipping the board. The New Game function encompasses resetting all game state elements, including board, history, clocks, and AI engine state, to prepare for a fresh game.\n\n\nFeature implementation requires separate focus for each functional area. Timer functionality necessitates managing configurable presets and applying time increments correctly with start/stop conditions, while enforcing game 